### Why does OFFSET get slower on deep pages?

The database cannot magically start at row ten thousand. It must produce the sorted stream, count through the first m rows, discard them all, and only then emit the next n.

```sql
-- Page 1: fast (discard 0 rows)
SELECT * FROM transactions
ORDER BY txn_id
LIMIT 10 OFFSET 0;

-- Page 1000: slow (discard 9990 rows)
SELECT * FROM transactions
ORDER BY txn_id
LIMIT 10 OFFSET 9990;
-- The DB reads and throws away 9990 rows every time this page is requested
```

```text
Page depth vs response time (OFFSET):
  Page 1:     2 ms    (discard 0 rows)
  Page 100:   45 ms   (discard 990 rows)
  Page 1000:  380 ms  (discard 9990 rows)
  Page 10000: 3800 ms (discard 99990 rows)
  Cost grows linearly: O(offset)
```

### How does keyset pagination work?

Instead of skipping rows, remember the sort key of the last row shown and ask for everything after it.

```sql
-- First page
SELECT * FROM transactions
WHERE account_id = 1001
ORDER BY txn_id
LIMIT 10;
-- Returns txn_id: 101, 102, ..., 110
-- Last seen: txn_id = 110

-- Next page (keyset)
SELECT * FROM transactions
WHERE account_id = 1001
  AND txn_id > 110  -- seek past the last seen key
ORDER BY txn_id
LIMIT 10;
-- Index seek to txn_id > 110, read 10 rows
-- Same speed on page 1 or page 10000
```

### Why is keyset O(log n)?

If `txn_id` is indexed, the database performs a B-tree descent to find the entry just after the cursor value and reads forward. It never touches the preceding rows.

```text
B-tree index on txn_id:
         ┌───────┐
         │  500  │
         └───┬───┘
       ┌─────┴─────┐
       │           │
    ┌──▼──┐    ┌──▼──┐
    │ 200 │    │ 800 │
    └──┬──┘    └─────┘
       │
    ┌──▼──┐
    │ 110 │ ◄── seek here for txn_id > 110
    └──┬──┘
       │
    Read next 10 leaf entries: 111, 112, ..., 120

Cost: O(log n) tree descent + O(page_size) leaf read
Same cost at any depth!
```

### How does OFFSET behave under concurrent inserts?

Inserting rows while a user paginates shifts everything.

```text
Offset pagination with concurrent inserts:
  Page 1: rows 1-10
  -- New row inserted at position 5 --
  Page 2: rows 11-20
  Row 10 appears AGAIN on page 2! (duplicate)

Keyset pagination with concurrent inserts:
  Page 1: rows 1-10, cursor = id 10
  -- New row inserted --
  Page 2: WHERE id > 10
  New rows after id 10 appear naturally, no duplicates
```

### How do you handle multi-column ordering?

For ordering by a timestamp with an id tie-breaker, use a composite cursor.

```sql
-- Composite cursor: (created_at, txn_id)
SELECT * FROM transactions
WHERE account_id = 1001
  AND (created_at, txn_id) > ('2026-06-15 10:00:00', 500)
ORDER BY created_at, txn_id
LIMIT 10;
```

This requires a composite index:

```sql
CREATE INDEX idx_txn_acct_date_id
    ON transactions (account_id, created_at, txn_id);
```

### How do you implement keyset pagination in Java?

Rajesh implements cursor-based pagination for the transaction history API at National Bank of India.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import java.sql.*;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class TransactionRepository {

    public record TransactionPage(
        List<Transaction> items, Long nextCursor
    ) {}

    public TransactionPage getPage(Connection conn, long accountId,
                                    Long afterTxnId, int pageSize)
            throws SQLException {
        String sql;
        if (afterTxnId == null) {
            sql = "SELECT txn_id, amount, created_at FROM transactions "
                + "WHERE account_id = ? ORDER BY txn_id LIMIT ?";
        } else {
            sql = "SELECT txn_id, amount, created_at FROM transactions "
                + "WHERE account_id = ? AND txn_id > ? ORDER BY txn_id LIMIT ?";
        }

        List<Transaction> items = new ArrayList<>();
        try (PreparedStatement ps = conn.prepareStatement(sql)) {
            int idx = 1;
            ps.setLong(idx++, accountId);
            if (afterTxnId != null) {
                ps.setLong(idx++, afterTxnId);
            }
            ps.setInt(idx, pageSize);

            try (ResultSet rs = ps.executeQuery()) {
                while (rs.next()) {
                    items.add(new Transaction(
                        rs.getLong("txn_id"),
                        rs.getBigDecimal("amount"),
                        rs.getTimestamp("created_at")
                    ));
                }
            }
        }

        Long nextCursor = items.isEmpty() ? null
            : items.get(items.size() - 1).txnId();
        return new TransactionPage(items, nextCursor);
    }

    public record Transaction(
        long txnId, java.math.BigDecimal amount, Timestamp createdAt
    ) {}
}
```

### How does the API expose the cursor?

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/accounts/{accountId}/transactions")
public class TransactionController {

    @GetMapping
    public TransactionRepository.TransactionPage listTransactions(
            @PathVariable long accountId,
            @RequestParam(required = false) Long after,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int pageSize) {
        return txnRepo.getPage(conn, accountId, after, pageSize);
    }
    // Client calls: GET /api/accounts/1001/transactions?after=500&pageSize=20
}
```

### What is the trade-off of keyset pagination?

Keyset gives next/previous navigation, not arbitrary jump-to-page-500. For infinite-scroll feeds, transaction histories, and APIs, that is exactly what you want.

| Feature | OFFSET | Keyset |
|---|---|---|
| Deep page speed | O(offset) | O(log n) |
| Concurrent insert stability | Duplicates/skips | Stable |
| Jump to page N | Yes | No (next/prev only) |
| Implementation | Simple | Requires cursor tracking |
| Best for | Small datasets, admin UI | Large datasets, APIs, feeds |

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The cursor must be on a unique, ordered column or a unique composite.
- A composite index matching the cursor columns is required for efficient seeks.
- For descending order, use `WHERE (created_at, id) < (:last_ts, :last_id)` and `ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC`.
- Consider encoding the cursor as an opaque base64 token so clients cannot tamper with it.
- GraphQL and Relay connections use this pattern with `after` and `first` parameters.
